  "body": "**Scope:** an independent source-binding check for open #540. No new \u03ba, and the selected record is unchanged.\n\n## Task card\n- **Card:** `formal-interface-lemma` (independent-verification track, chooser seed `89b099639256052efb5bbfd359684b0f`). Scope is **refined** to \"audit one theorem-to-source binding\".\n- **Base:** `49b3157d`. **Audited head:** #540 `acc1917034d10f464271ab115fda314fd4eee7a3` (`PhysicalSourceConnection.lean` blob `9a78d48f`, `generate_ownership_data.py` blob `63703310`).\n- **Question:** #540's `pendingTag` gives idle child 1 the axes `[15,22)` and child 2 the axes `[1,29)` by hand. Lean checks only that these ranges contain the targets, and the extractor hard-codes the bases 15 and 1. Are these the actual pending sets from the source schedule? This is the next task named in the [handoff](https://github.com/CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds/discussions/1166).\n\n## Result\nBoth ranges are **exactly** the derived pending sets.\n\n| Child | Pair | Levels | Window | Derived | #540 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 1 | 1\u21922 | {2} | E\u2082 = `[15,22)` | `[15,22)` |\n| 2 | 2 | \u22121\u21923 | {0,1,2,3} | q\u22a5\u2295E\u2081\u2295E\u2082\u2295E\u2083 = `[1,29)` | `[1,29)` |\n\n## Method\nThe checker is standard-library only and imports nothing from #434, #442 or #540.\n\n- The hosted template is bound to `bit_template_sha256` in #442's `EXPECTED.json` on main, and the geometry is bound to the template.\n- The exterior 7-block layout is read from all 120 ports' routes.\n- Each `[q/2 | A_q]` chart is checked to be exactly orthogonal.\n- Level model (from PR386): L\u208b\u2081 = span(q), L_\u2113 = Q\u2078\u2295E\u2081..E_\u2113. All 10 idle ranks are checked against it.\n- The windows are recomputed from the schedule.\n- All 747 hosted routes `(child, off, w)` are mapped to `pending[off:off+w]` and compared with #540's `base+off`.\n\nSix mutations are rejected: previous-level block, a range that includes the q axis, swapped children, L\u208b\u2081 = {}, an offset past the pending rank, and stage 2 removed from the window.\n\n## Not covered\n- This is a Python derivation, not a kernel proof.\n- The ranks fix only dimensions. Identifying L\u208b\u2081 with span(q) relies on PR386's route identity, which is not re-proved here.\n- The ascending order of axes within a child is a convention shared with #540's extractor; PR442 does not fix it.\n- Only one invocation is checked.\n\n## Reproduce\n```sh\npython3 -I -B research/pr540-pending-range-derivation/verify.py      # ~0.5 s\npython3.12 scripts/research_package.py --package research/pr540-pending-range-derivation\npython3 -B -m unittest discover -s tests -p 'test_research_*.py'     # 20 OK, 1 skipped\n```\nThe two vendored inputs are byte-identical to #540's `inputs/` files of the same names; the SHA256 pins are in `verify.py`.\n\n**Credit:** construction and hosting rule from #434/#442; bit geometry from PR386; the Lean formalization and gap identification are cheesegrader's #540. Prepared by Douglas Colkitt with substantial Claude (Anthropic) assistance. Apache-2.0.\n\n**Feedback:** the endpoint-moment card (`independent-moment`) is already covered for the selected record by three code-disjoint engines (Mercator, base-two, atanh prime chain). The handoff thread was a better source of an unmet independent check.\n\n\ud83e\udd16 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
